WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.842
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:07.112 --> 00:09.114
[theme music playing]

3
00:31.595 --> 00:33.430
[rain pattering]

4
00:33.514 --> 00:35.057
[thunder rumbling]

5
00:42.898 --> 00:43.941
[Song-yi] Chris.

6
00:49.613 --> 00:51.407
Can we talk for a minute?

7
00:55.411 --> 00:57.162
[breathes heavily]

8
01:05.296 --> 01:07.006
[intense music playing]

9
01:10.634 --> 01:11.635
Chris.

10
01:12.761 --> 01:14.638
She's Pyeongyeon Group's chairwoman.

11
01:15.514 --> 01:18.058
I had no idea she'd visit out of the blue like this.

12
01:20.686 --> 01:22.938
They came unannounced without reservation.

13
01:23.606 --> 01:24.690
I'll tell them to leave.

14
01:25.649 --> 01:28.777
The kitchen's closed right now, and we're out of ingredients anyway.

15
01:33.782 --> 01:34.783
No, wait.

16
01:37.995 --> 01:39.663
We'll serve them. Serve the wine.

17
01:40.539 --> 01:42.917
-What? -Tell them we're out of chicken today.

18
01:43.542 --> 01:44.877
And we'll serve fish instead.

19
01:44.960 --> 01:45.961
Chris.

20
01:46.045 --> 01:47.713
We'll have to make the sauce from scratch.

21
01:47.796 --> 01:49.840
-Chris! -[breathes deeply]

22
01:51.091 --> 01:52.885
They're customers who came to eat our food.

23
01:55.054 --> 01:56.513
I'll start on the appetizer.

24
02:11.946 --> 02:13.447
[exhales shakily]

25
02:24.792 --> 02:27.211
[Woo-jin] You set the storage room to the wrong temperature?

26
02:27.795 --> 02:28.796
So?

27
02:28.879 --> 02:31.340
FRANCE, 8 YEARS AGO

28
02:31.966 --> 02:33.133
It all went bad?

29
02:34.051 --> 02:35.302
All of it?

30
02:37.888 --> 02:38.889
[scoffs]

31
02:39.807 --> 02:43.102
[scoffs] You set it to ten degrees instead of three?

32
02:44.603 --> 02:45.604
All that…

33
02:46.647 --> 02:47.982
All that work to get it…

34
02:50.317 --> 02:52.194
Do you have any idea how much work that was?

35
02:53.570 --> 02:55.072
[grunts] Do you…

36
02:56.991 --> 02:58.283
Do you know what you have done?

37
03:00.327 --> 03:01.328
Get it.

38
03:02.538 --> 03:04.039
I don't care how. Just get it.

39
03:07.084 --> 03:08.252
[breathes shakily]

40
03:08.335 --> 03:10.379
Do whatever it takes to get it.

41
03:10.462 --> 03:11.714
Or else…

42
03:14.425 --> 03:16.301
I won't let you get away with this.

43
03:16.385 --> 03:17.386
[stammers] Yes, Chef.

44
03:18.178 --> 03:22.016
I'll go get it. I'll figure something out. I promise I won't let you down. I'm sorry.

45
03:22.099 --> 03:24.309
I'm so sorry. [breathes shakily]

46
03:27.646 --> 03:29.314
[breathes shakily]

47
03:30.482 --> 03:32.192
[door opens]

48
03:41.577 --> 03:42.578
Chef.

49
03:48.500 --> 03:52.629
According to the police findings, the accident was caused by speeding.

50
03:52.713 --> 03:54.631
Accidents happen there all the time,

51
03:54.715 --> 03:58.260
and there was heavy fog during that specific night of the accident too.

52
03:58.343 --> 03:59.386
[exhales sharply]

53
04:06.351 --> 04:07.352
[sighs]

54
04:38.634 --> 04:40.385
[dramatic music playing]

55
04:59.071 --> 05:00.823
[beeping]

56
05:16.380 --> 05:17.506
CHEF CHA

57
05:17.589 --> 05:19.299
-Oh. -[phone buzzing]

58
05:19.383 --> 05:20.551
Go on, take it.

59
05:23.971 --> 05:24.972
Hey, Woo-jin.

60
05:25.848 --> 05:26.849
You busy?

61
05:27.724 --> 05:28.725
Hmm?

62
05:30.602 --> 05:32.312
[sniffles, sighs]

63
05:33.647 --> 05:35.065
If you're finished, wanna come over?

64
05:36.817 --> 05:38.861
I'm having a drink at the bar.

65
05:38.944 --> 05:40.154
Alone?

66
05:40.237 --> 05:41.864
Yeah. [sniffs]

67
05:42.573 --> 05:44.491
-Um… -Okay. Go ahead.

68
05:44.575 --> 05:45.576
[chuckles]

69
05:53.792 --> 05:56.628
Uh, sorry. I don't think I can come over tonight.

70
05:57.379 --> 06:00.090
I'm having dinner with the chairwoman right now.

71
06:01.341 --> 06:02.426
Having dinner?

72
06:03.677 --> 06:05.053
Really, this late at night?

73
06:07.848 --> 06:08.891
Where?

74
06:10.309 --> 06:13.562
Oh, um… we're at…

75
06:16.648 --> 06:18.066
Famiglia.

76
06:18.150 --> 06:20.152
She said she wanted to come here.

77
06:26.658 --> 06:27.659
Okay. I got it.

78
06:28.368 --> 06:29.828
-[phone beeps] -[sighs]

79
06:55.354 --> 06:56.647
[chuckles]

80
06:56.730 --> 06:59.358
I was a little worried because the theme was a bit vague,

81
06:59.858 --> 07:02.194
but seeing how you tied it into the challenge,

82
07:02.277 --> 07:04.738
I found the combination surprisingly interesting.

83
07:04.821 --> 07:06.448
Right. [chuckles]

84
07:06.531 --> 07:09.868
But it won't be easy to keep coming up with ideas like this.

85
07:09.952 --> 07:13.288
Are you sure you can manage? How are you keeping it secret?

86
07:14.081 --> 07:17.084
We prepared several times as many themes as we need,

87
07:17.167 --> 07:19.086
and the challenge for each round is decided

88
07:19.169 --> 07:21.546
at a secret location two days before filming.

89
07:22.256 --> 07:25.550
Only the main judges and I know, so there's no need to worry.

90
07:25.634 --> 07:28.011
Good. Make sure you stick to that rule.

91
07:28.595 --> 07:31.765
Especially make sure nothing ever leaks to Ms. Baek.

92
07:31.848 --> 07:33.809
And of course, don't tell me either.

93
07:33.892 --> 07:35.852
[chuckles] I'll keep that in mind.

94
07:37.896 --> 07:38.897
So…

95
07:39.523 --> 07:41.400
what audience do you expect for the premiere?

96
07:45.237 --> 07:46.530
Of course,

97
07:46.613 --> 07:50.284
I'm sure the final numbers are way more important to you than the premiere.

98
07:50.367 --> 07:53.120
-[chuckles] -All right. Let's see how this turns out.

99
07:53.203 --> 07:56.206
Whether it'll be a one-out reliever or earn us a relief win.

100
07:58.375 --> 07:59.376
[chuckles]

101
08:17.477 --> 08:20.647
I mean, it's ridiculous enough that he's cooking

102
08:20.731 --> 08:22.441
for people who showed up after closing.

103
08:22.524 --> 08:24.026
Does he have to put so much effort into it?

104
08:24.109 --> 08:27.612
And they're the very people who pulled all kinds of crap to ruin Famiglia…

105
08:31.033 --> 08:33.285
[sighs deeply]

106
08:33.368 --> 08:36.621
If it were me, I'd throw in some poisonous mushrooms.

107
08:37.581 --> 08:39.249
Or maybe even salmonella.

108
08:41.376 --> 08:43.754
Thinking about my brother makes my blood boil.

109
08:43.837 --> 08:45.255
Don't, Chef.

110
08:46.548 --> 08:47.549
Look.

111
08:48.550 --> 08:49.843
I'm not like you, Chris.

112
08:49.926 --> 08:53.013
I can't be all cool and American about this. [sighs]

113
08:54.389 --> 08:56.850
[door opens, closes]

114
09:12.491 --> 09:13.492
[gasps]

115
09:14.534 --> 09:16.995
Wow. What is this?

116
09:17.621 --> 09:21.875
It's definitely tomato-based, but it's so smooth. Wow.

117
09:26.463 --> 09:28.757
How do I put it? It's earthy.

118
09:29.966 --> 09:31.718
It's not quite nutty.

119
09:31.802 --> 09:33.261
[chuckles] It's earthy.

120
09:47.234 --> 09:48.860
Mmm. [chuckles]

121
09:51.655 --> 09:56.618
Oh, you see… this dish isn't actually part of our current menu.

122
09:57.619 --> 10:00.831
I think the fish is a John Dory. [chuckles]

123
10:00.914 --> 10:03.166
But I'd really like to know how you made the sauce.

124
10:03.250 --> 10:06.378
Oh, the sauce is made with finely chopped dried pollock,

125
10:06.461 --> 10:09.965
tomatoes, glutinous rice gochujang, special homemade makjang,

126
10:10.048 --> 10:12.175
and a little bit of heavy cream to thicken it.

127
10:12.259 --> 10:13.969
Ah.

128
10:14.052 --> 10:15.804
[Song-yi] And the fish isn't just grilled either.

129
10:15.887 --> 10:19.349
It's pan-fried, then it is lightly smoked over applewood.

130
10:20.142 --> 10:22.936
I recommend putting some fish on your spoon first,

131
10:23.019 --> 10:26.314
then topping it with the tomato and glutinous rice gochujang sauce.

132
10:26.940 --> 10:27.941
Enjoy.

133
10:44.749 --> 10:46.293
[gentle music playing]

134
10:54.926 --> 10:57.220
[emotional music playing]

135
11:22.329 --> 11:24.498
[classical music playing on stereo]

136
11:27.834 --> 11:29.794
-Mr. Park. -Yes, ma'am.

137
11:31.546 --> 11:33.548
Let's turn the music off for a moment.

138
11:34.799 --> 11:37.385
[exhales heavily] I feel like riding in silence tonight.

139
11:37.928 --> 11:39.554
Of course. I'll turn it off.

140
11:42.891 --> 11:44.267
[music stops]

141
11:44.893 --> 11:47.312
[Jeong-won] Why do you always work as a sous-chef?

142
11:49.147 --> 11:53.360
Even though you're more than capable of becoming an exceptional head chef?

143
11:53.443 --> 11:56.696
Probably because it's easier. There's less responsibility.

144
11:56.780 --> 11:59.824
A sous-chef doesn't have to worry about the restaurant's sales, for example.

145
11:59.908 --> 12:01.201
[chuckles]

146
12:02.118 --> 12:03.954
What a textbook answer. Come on.

147
12:06.748 --> 12:08.250
To keep myself unburdened.

148
12:09.834 --> 12:11.503
That way, I can travel around freely

149
12:11.586 --> 12:13.880
and learn different things in different places.

150
12:17.717 --> 12:18.718
[sighs]

151
12:25.100 --> 12:29.312
I don't want people complimenting me while subtly looking down on Asians,

152
12:30.063 --> 12:32.482
and then grovel to them for a head chef position.

153
12:34.818 --> 12:36.778
I decided it wasn't the right time

154
12:37.487 --> 12:39.864
to open up a restaurant under my own name.

155
12:43.034 --> 12:44.119
I believed

156
12:44.953 --> 12:46.329
I still had more to learn.

157
12:49.332 --> 12:51.209
There are plenty of reasons I could give.

158
12:52.043 --> 12:54.296
It's just difficult to say which one is the real one.

159
12:56.631 --> 12:57.632
[chuckles]

160
12:58.633 --> 13:00.510
Are you happy? With my reason?

161
13:04.472 --> 13:06.433
[Jeong-won] Why did you come back to Korea?

162
13:07.726 --> 13:09.936
Did you come after your birth parents?

163
13:11.813 --> 13:13.398
Uh, when did you come to the States?

164
13:13.481 --> 13:16.610
Oh, and tell me, what do you remember about Korea?

165
13:16.693 --> 13:19.487
I heard you're under contract with a restaurant in New York.

166
13:19.571 --> 13:21.114
Why enter this competition when you know

167
13:21.197 --> 13:23.491
you could get into trouble for breach of contract?

168
13:23.575 --> 13:25.827
And why are you representing Famiglia?

169
13:25.910 --> 13:27.704
[blows raspberry, chuckles]

170
13:27.787 --> 13:32.417
Oh. [chuckles] Sorry. Too many questions, right?

171
13:33.209 --> 13:35.170
[sighs] Just one.

172
13:36.338 --> 13:39.299
Ask me one question. That's all I'll answer for today.

173
13:40.675 --> 13:42.010
I wanna get some sleep.

174
13:43.261 --> 13:45.180
-[clicks tongue] -[chuckles]

175
13:46.681 --> 13:48.058
Okay then.

176
13:48.141 --> 13:49.851
Deal. Just one.

177
13:55.774 --> 13:57.275
Is there someone you love?

178
14:00.945 --> 14:01.946
Tell me, Chris.

179
14:03.365 --> 14:04.699
Is there someone you love?

180
14:11.039 --> 14:13.041
[emotional music playing]

181
14:18.672 --> 14:19.673
My dad.

182
14:19.756 --> 14:20.799
What?

183
14:22.676 --> 14:23.677
My father.

184
14:26.429 --> 14:27.931
The person I love the most,

185
14:28.932 --> 14:30.767
I respect the most,

186
14:31.851 --> 14:33.603
the one I miss the most

187
14:34.688 --> 14:35.689
is my father.

188
14:37.065 --> 14:38.233
Oh.

189
14:39.109 --> 14:42.070
Do you mean your American adoptive father?

190
14:42.570 --> 14:43.613
My birth father.

191
14:44.280 --> 14:45.490
[inhales deeply]

192
14:45.573 --> 14:47.325
The man who taught me how to cook.

193
14:48.702 --> 14:49.953
The man who made me who I am.

194
14:50.704 --> 14:53.581
Did your biological father also work as a chef?

195
14:54.457 --> 14:56.334
Then is he still working as a cook--

196
14:56.418 --> 14:58.211
I could never compare myself to him.

197
14:58.795 --> 15:00.463
My father's an exceptional chef.

198
15:01.423 --> 15:06.386
Actually, if I'm to be fair to him, he's the best in the world.

199
15:08.638 --> 15:10.557
I grew up eating his cooking,

200
15:11.182 --> 15:13.893
while learning to cook from him at the same time.

201
15:13.977 --> 15:17.313
We traveled through the mountains and fields, learning new flavors.

202
15:18.440 --> 15:20.275
My father always used to tell me

203
15:21.526 --> 15:22.694
that cooking isn't

204
15:23.570 --> 15:26.573
simply about making something delicious for someone to eat.

205
15:29.659 --> 15:33.288
My father, he doesn't make delicious food.

206
15:35.623 --> 15:39.836
My father makes food that moves people's hearts.

207
15:42.797 --> 15:44.257
[chuckles]

208
15:44.340 --> 15:46.801
It's my dream… [sniffles]

209
15:46.885 --> 15:49.053
…to become a chef my father would acknowledge.

210
15:51.097 --> 15:52.474
If I could just do that…

211
15:53.141 --> 15:54.434
If only I could…

212
15:56.686 --> 15:59.314
make something worthy of my father's approval…

213
16:01.733 --> 16:03.067
[chuckles]

214
16:06.988 --> 16:08.490
Where is he now?

215
16:11.284 --> 16:12.702
Where's your father?

216
16:18.708 --> 16:20.543
He's always with me.

217
16:30.261 --> 16:31.638
Even at this moment,

218
16:32.347 --> 16:34.140
I'm sure he's somewhere close by.

219
16:38.144 --> 16:39.896
[sniffles, inhales sharply]

220
16:49.155 --> 16:50.615
[rock music playing]

221
17:10.134 --> 17:11.177
Hey, what's up?

222
17:11.261 --> 17:12.846
I thought you had a drink last night.

223
17:12.929 --> 17:14.806
You have wild ginseng instead of alcohol or what?

224
17:15.682 --> 17:17.976
[panting]

225
17:38.538 --> 17:39.539
[chuckles]

226
17:40.707 --> 17:41.875
[sighs]

227
17:49.757 --> 17:52.468
What happened last night? You hardly ever drink much.

228
17:53.094 --> 17:55.805
[chuckles] At least I get to have hangover soup.

229
17:55.889 --> 17:56.890
[Woo-jin chuckles]

230
18:02.186 --> 18:06.024
Listen, is it because you lost to Chris K. again?

231
18:08.526 --> 18:09.527
[Woo-jin] Wait.

232
18:15.742 --> 18:17.035
So you knew?

233
18:19.037 --> 18:20.038
Is that… [sighs]

234
18:21.289 --> 18:22.624
…why you brought him in?

235
18:24.208 --> 18:25.501
To get me to join?

236
18:25.585 --> 18:26.669
Listen now.

237
18:27.670 --> 18:30.173
I still believe you will be the one who'll win the competition.

238
18:31.090 --> 18:33.509
I don't care about the premiere's ratings.

239
18:33.593 --> 18:34.677
It's always

240
18:35.970 --> 18:38.056
the final ratings that really matter.

241
18:42.810 --> 18:43.811
Woo-jin, look,

242
18:44.729 --> 18:47.023
I trust you'll give us a spectacular finale.

243
18:50.777 --> 18:51.778
Listen.

244
18:52.570 --> 18:56.282
[gasps softly]

245
18:56.366 --> 18:57.492
I am Cha Woo-jin…

246
18:59.494 --> 19:02.121
and I won't let that punk win the competition.

247
19:15.468 --> 19:17.345
[dramatic music playing]

248
19:31.693 --> 19:34.529
[child coughing]

249
19:34.612 --> 19:39.158
All right. Yes, there you go. That's it. Here.

250
19:40.284 --> 19:42.120
Here, have some water first.

251
19:42.996 --> 19:45.373
It's warm, so sip it slowly.

252
19:49.002 --> 19:50.420
Here.

253
19:55.758 --> 19:57.385
There we go. Let me take that.

254
19:58.386 --> 19:59.637
All done.

255
20:04.851 --> 20:05.852
[groans]

256
20:07.228 --> 20:08.521
-All down? Hmm? -Mm-hmm.

257
20:10.189 --> 20:11.482
Okay, let's lie down.

258
20:15.570 --> 20:20.116
I promise. She'll be here soon. So hang tight, okay?

259
20:20.199 --> 20:21.242
Uh-huh.

260
20:22.744 --> 20:23.745
Dad.

261
20:24.370 --> 20:26.706
Please don't worry about me.

262
20:26.789 --> 20:29.167
Make lots of yummy food today.

263
20:29.709 --> 20:31.753
Okay, Dad?

264
20:33.796 --> 20:34.797
[chuckles]

265
20:35.715 --> 20:37.216
[Je-hyeon] I wonder,

266
20:37.300 --> 20:41.012
when was the last time she really smiled? It's been so long.

267
20:41.846 --> 20:44.599
I have… I have a young daughter. Um…

268
20:44.682 --> 20:46.142
PARK JE-HYEON OWNER-CHEF OF BINGSAK

269
20:46.225 --> 20:49.479
She means more to me than anything else in the entire world.

270
20:50.104 --> 20:51.355
My daughter, she…

271
20:53.066 --> 20:54.067
[clicks tongue]

272
20:54.567 --> 20:56.736
She has a very serious heart condition.

273
20:57.653 --> 21:00.198
My wife passed away from the same exact illness.

274
21:08.539 --> 21:09.582
BINGSAK

275
21:09.665 --> 21:11.959
BINGSAK

276
21:12.043 --> 21:13.419
I moved to Jeju Island,

277
21:13.503 --> 21:18.216
and then changed my cooking style to a healthy, Mediterranean-inspired approach

278
21:18.299 --> 21:20.927
that brings out the natural flavors of the ingredients,

279
21:21.010 --> 21:22.553
and I did it for my daughter.

280
21:23.888 --> 21:25.473
Nothing makes me happier

281
21:25.556 --> 21:29.685
than seeing my daughter enjoy the healthy meals I cook for her.

282
21:32.563 --> 21:34.023
I named the restaurant "Bingsak"

283
21:35.024 --> 21:38.903
because I wanna see my daughter smile, even just one more time.

284
21:40.696 --> 21:44.575
In the Jeju dialect, bingsak means "a bright smile."

285
21:50.248 --> 21:53.543
[Ok-seon] I said the millstone. Who told you to use the blender?

286
21:54.252 --> 21:57.880
Haven't I told you over and over that the heat of the motor makes it bitter?

287
21:57.964 --> 21:59.423
JANG OK-SEON OWNER-CHEF OF ONGOJISIM

288
21:59.507 --> 22:00.675
Did you do it on purpose?

289
22:03.970 --> 22:06.347
You're cutting corners just to save a few minutes?

290
22:06.931 --> 22:09.392
If you don't want to do it, say so. I won't force you to.

291
22:09.475 --> 22:12.937
Uh, I'm sorry, Chef. Sorry, it was my mistake.

292
22:13.980 --> 22:15.565
I've told you many times!

293
22:16.274 --> 22:17.358
Tradition

294
22:18.609 --> 22:19.694
is devotion.

295
22:21.279 --> 22:22.405
Yes. Thank you, Chef.

296
22:26.075 --> 22:28.244
[chattering]

297
22:31.455 --> 22:32.498
Five million.

298
22:34.667 --> 22:37.044
Your five, plus five.

299
22:38.212 --> 22:40.381
[tense music playing]

300
22:43.426 --> 22:44.427
[Ja-ryong] Call.

301
22:45.928 --> 22:47.680
Full house. [laughs]

302
22:48.973 --> 22:50.224
Ah. [chuckles]

303
22:50.308 --> 22:52.935
Wow, I won at last. [chuckles]

304
22:53.019 --> 22:55.938
-I got four nines. -[cackles]

305
22:58.691 --> 23:01.110
Oh, thank you very much. Yes.

306
23:01.194 --> 23:02.612
What kind of game is this?

307
23:02.695 --> 23:04.822
I can't even win with a full house? Damn it!

308
23:05.656 --> 23:07.200
Well done.

309
23:07.283 --> 23:09.493
Geez. [growls]

310
23:11.829 --> 23:12.830
Damn.

311
23:13.831 --> 23:15.291
[breathes deeply]

312
23:16.334 --> 23:20.129
YANG JA-RYONG HEAD CHEF OF GOLDEN DRAGON

313
23:21.005 --> 23:25.009
Hello. You know how much money you've borrowed from us so far, right?

314
23:25.092 --> 23:27.637
[sucks teeth] As of today,

315
23:28.304 --> 23:30.598
you owe us 378 million won.

316
23:31.682 --> 23:33.267
-I know. -[gangster chuckles]

317
23:33.351 --> 23:36.604
So yeah, could you just… [clicks tongue] …give me some more time?

318
23:39.232 --> 23:41.734
[inhales sharply] I gave you my word, didn't I?

319
23:41.817 --> 23:44.487
Once I win the competition, I'll pay you back right away

320
23:44.570 --> 23:45.863
with the prize money.

321
23:45.947 --> 23:48.199
-So please just-- -Yes, of course.

322
23:49.033 --> 23:52.411
If you win, you pay me back that very day. "If" you win.

323
23:52.995 --> 23:54.705
But you see… [clicks tongue]

324
23:56.374 --> 23:57.583
You can win, right?

325
23:58.668 --> 23:59.669
Right?

326
24:00.419 --> 24:01.837
Because if you don't…

327
24:06.300 --> 24:11.055
I'll squeeze every last drop of juice out of this fat dumpling in no time.

328
24:12.223 --> 24:14.725
[whirring]

329
24:19.313 --> 24:20.648
Are the duxelles ready yet?

330
24:20.731 --> 24:21.732
It's ready, Chef.

331
24:21.816 --> 24:24.235
-Meringue. -Yes, Chef. It's done.

332
24:25.111 --> 24:27.405
-Michael, bring me the pastry bag. -Yes, Chef.

333
24:34.203 --> 24:36.747
It needs more color. Don't let it burn at the sides.

334
24:37.373 --> 24:38.708
Chef Geon-woo, let's see the carrots.

335
24:40.668 --> 24:41.919
-Cook a little longer. -Yes, Chef.

336
24:44.630 --> 24:47.675
Okay, that's good enough. Put it in the oven. It'll take a while.

337
24:47.758 --> 24:49.010
[Du-yeong] Yes, Chef.

338
24:49.093 --> 24:51.345
[Han] Chef Geon-woo, don't squeeze out too much moisture when you blend it.

339
24:51.429 --> 24:53.306
-[Geon-woo] Okay. -Okay. You're all doing great.

340
24:53.389 --> 24:54.765
Let's just pick up the pace a little.

341
24:54.849 --> 24:55.850
-Yes, Chef! -Yes, Chef!

342
25:00.354 --> 25:02.857
[phone buzzing]

343
25:06.360 --> 25:07.486
[Yoon-Jeong] It's only been 30 minutes,

344
25:07.570 --> 25:09.697
and there are already over 7,000 live comments.

345
25:09.780 --> 25:11.198
This is really taking off, isn't it?

346
25:11.282 --> 25:14.035
[Hee-min] Wow, the chefs look even better on TV.

347
25:14.118 --> 25:15.911
There's just something about men in uniform.

348
25:15.995 --> 25:17.163
[So-ha] Everyone's already saying

349
25:17.246 --> 25:19.874
Chef Cha Woo-jin will win this round hands down.

350
25:20.499 --> 25:21.584
[phone buzzing]

351
25:23.377 --> 25:26.088
PYEONGYEON GROUP CHAIRWOMAN MOON HAE-SEON

352
25:26.172 --> 25:28.382
[Hae-seon] Well done. It looks great.

353
25:28.466 --> 25:31.427
The ratings are going to take off from the premiere. I'd bet on it.

354
25:32.136 --> 25:33.137
However…

355
25:35.389 --> 25:37.558
Team Famiglia keeps catching my eye.

356
25:37.641 --> 25:39.852
You're not deliberately making them look good, are you?

357
25:39.935 --> 25:41.228
Kidding.

358
25:41.312 --> 25:42.313
[chuckles]

359
25:43.773 --> 25:45.941
SPLENDOR IN THE GRASS

360
25:48.736 --> 25:50.946
"Splendor in the Grass."

361
25:51.781 --> 25:54.450
This is the theme you have to follow in Round Two.

362
25:55.117 --> 25:57.036
Is this one throwing you for a loop too?

363
25:58.204 --> 26:00.247
Still not sure what to make of it?

364
26:00.331 --> 26:03.709
But we all know you will get it. Eventually.

365
26:04.335 --> 26:07.755
Your creative and artistic interpretations will rule!

366
26:11.634 --> 26:13.511
Your dazzling, yet moving dishes…

367
26:16.305 --> 26:18.849
I look forward to what you'll come up with this time.

368
26:22.520 --> 26:24.522
-[upbeat music playing] -[audience applauding]

369
26:24.605 --> 26:26.857
ROUND 1 COMPLETE

370
26:36.492 --> 26:39.954
ROUND 2 THEME SPLENDOR IN THE GRASS

371
26:48.879 --> 26:49.880
Right.

372
26:50.381 --> 26:51.382
Here.

373
26:52.591 --> 26:53.592
All together now!

374
26:54.301 --> 26:56.554
Bottoms up! Now!

375
26:56.637 --> 26:58.764
-Cheers! -[all] Cheers!

376
27:03.644 --> 27:05.688
Ah! Oh!

377
27:05.771 --> 27:07.648
[sighs]

378
27:07.731 --> 27:09.442
[Du-yeong] Oh, come on.

379
27:09.525 --> 27:11.986
You have to down the first glass. That's the rule.

380
27:12.611 --> 27:14.196
What's wrong with you?

381
27:15.948 --> 27:16.949
Oh.

382
27:17.741 --> 27:19.034
Oh! Whoo!

383
27:19.118 --> 27:21.078
[clapping]

384
27:24.623 --> 27:26.167
[Du-yeong whoops]

385
27:26.250 --> 27:28.627
-[all laughing] -Wow.

386
27:29.462 --> 27:33.299
By the way, guys, what do you think the theme for this round means?

387
27:33.382 --> 27:35.926
Honestly, the last one was hard, but at least…

388
27:36.010 --> 27:38.804
we had two lines of a poem to work with. [chuckles]

389
27:38.888 --> 27:41.891
[sighs] I mean, is this a writing test or a cooking competition?

390
27:41.974 --> 27:43.601
Seriously… [mutters]

391
27:44.101 --> 27:46.061
[sighs] I know.

392
27:46.145 --> 27:48.981
At this rate, they'll have us taking the CSAT next.

393
27:49.565 --> 27:52.651
"Splendor in the Grass."

394
27:53.652 --> 27:54.653
[sighs]

395
27:54.737 --> 27:57.114
Are we supposed to ride horses across a meadow?

396
27:58.365 --> 27:59.700
Wait.

397
27:59.783 --> 28:01.785
Or do we have to cook with horse meat? Huh?

398
28:01.869 --> 28:04.079
[horse whinnying, blustering]

399
28:04.163 --> 28:05.164
No.

400
28:05.247 --> 28:07.166
Then what's the "splendor" supposed to mean?

401
28:08.000 --> 28:09.001
[exhales deeply]

402
28:10.586 --> 28:11.587
Hmm?

403
28:13.839 --> 28:15.049
[Song-yi] Mmm.

404
28:19.136 --> 28:23.557
[chuckles] Nah. Why worry? We have Chris right here, right?

405
28:26.185 --> 28:27.228
Shall we eat?

406
28:33.025 --> 28:34.568
[whirring]

407
28:40.658 --> 28:41.825
It's so hard.

408
28:46.413 --> 28:47.581
Same here.

409
28:49.792 --> 28:50.960
It's hard.

410
28:54.713 --> 28:56.549
But we have no choice.

411
29:00.386 --> 29:01.804
Someone has to do it.

412
29:04.557 --> 29:05.641
Right?

413
29:09.395 --> 29:11.063
Famiglia isn't what's important.

414
29:22.366 --> 29:23.784
If not me…

415
29:27.663 --> 29:29.331
who else can do this?

416
29:33.210 --> 29:34.295
If not us…

417
29:37.965 --> 29:39.258
who else can do this?

418
29:55.566 --> 29:57.276
-[Hyeon-ju whistling] -Great work.

419
29:57.359 --> 29:58.861
[Hyeon-ju] Thank you.

420
29:58.944 --> 30:01.989
-So, how did the meeting go? -Hmm, it went well.

421
30:02.698 --> 30:03.699
What a relief.

422
30:03.782 --> 30:07.244
The ratings went straight up, bang! Right from the start!

423
30:07.328 --> 30:10.247
Honestly, you've got to give us Koreans credit.

424
30:10.331 --> 30:11.832
We really do love food, don't we?

425
30:12.666 --> 30:14.710
Oh, gosh, it was so entertaining.

426
30:14.793 --> 30:16.795
Seeing all those flashy dishes was such a treat. [chuckles]

427
30:16.879 --> 30:20.966
Right? Gotta tell you, Jeong-won really knows how to get the job done.

428
30:21.050 --> 30:22.176
Thanks to her… [sucks teeth]

429
30:22.259 --> 30:24.970
…I got to save face at the executive meeting.

430
30:25.054 --> 30:26.680
[laughing]

431
30:26.764 --> 30:27.848
By the way,

432
30:27.931 --> 30:32.394
what on earth does "Splendor in the Grass" mean?

433
30:34.146 --> 30:35.147
Jeon,

434
30:36.607 --> 30:37.608
what do you think?

435
30:38.192 --> 30:39.610
-Well… -Yes?

436
30:39.693 --> 30:41.695
I have absolutely no idea either.

437
30:42.946 --> 30:44.782
The viewers seem curious too.

438
30:44.865 --> 30:47.034
People are speculating, making all kinds of guesses.

439
30:47.576 --> 30:48.952
The response has been huge.

440
30:49.036 --> 30:50.287
Hmm.

441
30:50.371 --> 30:52.665
I was worried people might find it too difficult,

442
30:52.748 --> 30:54.833
so that's a relief.

443
30:56.418 --> 30:57.753
But wait.

444
30:57.836 --> 31:00.255
I know secrecy is of the most importance,

445
31:00.339 --> 31:03.258
but shouldn't they at least give me, the CEO, a heads-up?

446
31:03.342 --> 31:06.178
I mean, the subsidiary CEOs kept asking me about it,

447
31:06.261 --> 31:07.930
and all I could say was, "I don't know. I don't know."

448
31:08.013 --> 31:09.264
It was humiliating!

449
31:09.348 --> 31:11.850
[stammers] I-I know, right?

450
31:11.934 --> 31:15.479
I mean, shouldn't you as the CEO at least know what the theme means?

451
31:15.562 --> 31:17.064
-That way you could-- -Well, of course!

452
31:17.773 --> 31:20.693
They're keeping it secret from the chairwoman, so I really can't complain.

453
31:20.776 --> 31:22.194
-Right. -[gasps]

454
31:23.112 --> 31:24.697
Two days before they shot that episode,

455
31:24.780 --> 31:27.324
I took Ms. Seon and the judges to that place.

456
31:27.408 --> 31:29.159
You know, the one I go to all the time.

457
31:29.243 --> 31:31.078
-Oh, Ch-Chowon-- [chuckles] -Right, Chowon Chambokguk.

458
31:31.161 --> 31:32.204
I treated them to dinner there,

459
31:32.287 --> 31:36.959
and they all fell in love with the clear puffer fish soup! Aha!

460
31:37.042 --> 31:39.712
So that's why it's "Splendor in the Grass," right?

461
31:41.213 --> 31:42.464
Or maybe not?

462
31:42.548 --> 31:43.716
Oh, I don't know!

463
31:43.799 --> 31:45.801
[footsteps receding]

464
31:50.264 --> 31:51.432
What?

465
31:52.224 --> 31:53.350
A puffer fish, you say?

466
31:58.731 --> 31:59.940
That's right, In-seop.

467
32:00.524 --> 32:03.235
"Splendor in the Grass" is from Chowon Chambokguk.

468
32:05.028 --> 32:07.656
Hmm. [whispering] "Splendor in the Grass."

469
32:10.284 --> 32:13.787
Ms. Baek mentioned she took Ms. Song and the judges there,

470
32:13.871 --> 32:16.206
on the very day they were choosing the theme last week.

471
32:16.290 --> 32:18.834
Apparently, they all raved about the food they served there.

472
32:19.877 --> 32:23.839
And given that the theme "Splendor in the Grass" was finalized that afternoon,

473
32:23.922 --> 32:27.551
it's likely they got the idea from Chowon Chambokguk.

474
32:28.051 --> 32:31.346
The restaurant, it's one of Ms. Baek's regular spots.

475
32:31.430 --> 32:32.931
And it's super famous too.

476
32:34.808 --> 32:35.976
-Chef Yoo. -Yes?

477
32:38.979 --> 32:40.439
Try not to overthink it.

478
32:41.190 --> 32:44.610
The professionals often defy expectations with something simple.

479
32:45.694 --> 32:49.198
You know who Ms. Baek is. She's CEO of Pyeongyeon Media.

480
32:50.616 --> 32:52.117
She knows what she's saying.

481
32:54.870 --> 32:57.664
Puffer fish is the main ingredient.

482
33:01.418 --> 33:03.295
Cook something with puffer fish.

483
33:05.422 --> 33:06.423
Puffer fish.

484
33:08.091 --> 33:09.384
[Hyeon-ju] No!

485
33:09.468 --> 33:11.178
That's not it!

486
33:13.180 --> 33:14.181
Damn it.

487
33:15.474 --> 33:19.853
I told you to look into his background. Did I ask for a dissertation, huh?

488
33:20.521 --> 33:24.191
That bastard's approach to cooking. His culinary philosophy. His grades.

489
33:24.274 --> 33:25.859
His reputation in the industry.

490
33:25.943 --> 33:28.904
The fact that he only ever works as a sous-chef or whatever.

491
33:28.987 --> 33:30.948
None of that matters to me!

492
33:31.031 --> 33:32.157
Damn it!

493
33:32.825 --> 33:35.619
-[screams] Damn it! -[gasps] My apologies.

494
33:35.702 --> 33:36.745
My apologies.

495
33:46.421 --> 33:47.422
[chokes]

496
33:47.506 --> 33:49.800
What's the most entertaining TV genre?

497
33:50.509 --> 33:52.177
What's the most entertaining TV genre?

498
33:52.261 --> 33:53.720
[strained] Action?

499
33:53.804 --> 33:56.723
A trashy soap opera. A trashy soap opera.

500
33:56.807 --> 33:58.392
Birth secrets,

501
33:58.475 --> 34:01.311
forbidden love, revenge, affairs.

502
34:01.395 --> 34:03.313
Is this bastard an orphan? Who are his real parents?

503
34:03.397 --> 34:06.441
Is the woman he is falling for actually his sister?

504
34:06.525 --> 34:08.443
Maybe it turns out he put a fake mole on his face,

505
34:08.527 --> 34:10.863
or even went under the knife to change his appearance for revenge?

506
34:10.946 --> 34:14.283
Or maybe he's being blackmailed over something he did in the past?

507
34:14.366 --> 34:18.287
Or maybe he really did kill someone years ago?

508
34:18.370 --> 34:19.830
Ms. Baek… [gagging]

509
34:19.913 --> 34:22.374
That's what makes for real entertainment.

510
34:22.457 --> 34:24.126
A trashy soap opera!

511
34:25.168 --> 34:26.169
[gasps]

512
34:28.297 --> 34:30.716
[breathing heavily]

513
34:31.675 --> 34:32.801
[coughs]

514
34:35.137 --> 34:37.264
[panting]

515
34:39.600 --> 34:41.351
That's the stuff I'm interested in.

516
34:41.435 --> 34:42.853
And that's…

517
34:43.729 --> 34:47.274
What people want to see too. A soap opera.

518
34:47.357 --> 34:48.442
Yes.

519
34:49.443 --> 34:51.945
[eerie music playing]

520
34:52.029 --> 34:53.989
[Jeong-won] Chris K.'s childhood?

521
34:54.072 --> 34:56.533
Uh, I'm not sure.

522
34:56.617 --> 34:59.620
I don't really know anything about Chris K.'s childhood either.

523
34:59.703 --> 35:01.038
There are no records.

524
35:01.872 --> 35:05.083
He doesn't really like talking about his childhood.

525
35:05.167 --> 35:06.168
I see.

526
35:07.628 --> 35:09.004
Then…

527
35:09.087 --> 35:12.007
there's no way to find out when he went to the States either?

528
35:12.966 --> 35:14.509
No. Not that either.

529
35:16.929 --> 35:18.138
Ah.

530
35:19.306 --> 35:22.017
He did mention his father a few times, though.

531
35:24.144 --> 35:25.145
His father?

532
35:26.605 --> 35:27.814
What did he say?

533
35:28.690 --> 35:30.108
Does he live with his father?

534
35:32.611 --> 35:34.655
[footsteps approaching]

535
35:35.364 --> 35:37.866
Oh. Hello. Huh. You're early.

536
35:37.950 --> 35:39.201
-Oh, you made it. -Hello.

537
35:39.284 --> 35:41.870
It took me an hour just to get out of Seoul.

538
35:42.871 --> 35:44.122
Seoul traffic is really

539
35:44.873 --> 35:45.916
unpredictable.

540
35:45.999 --> 35:47.918
Thank you for coming all this way.

541
35:48.001 --> 35:51.338
I know you're all busy, so shall we get started right away?

542
35:59.972 --> 36:01.723
[knocking on door quietly]

543
36:03.141 --> 36:04.393
[knocking continues]

544
36:23.453 --> 36:24.454
Song.

545
36:26.999 --> 36:28.000
Song.

546
36:28.083 --> 36:30.168
[sighs]

547
36:43.724 --> 36:45.308
[grunting softly]

548
36:54.860 --> 36:55.861
Chris?

549
36:56.778 --> 36:58.405
[gentle music playing]

550
36:58.488 --> 37:00.699
FAMIGLIA

551
37:00.782 --> 37:01.825
[yawning]

552
37:01.908 --> 37:03.827
What could be so precious

553
37:03.910 --> 37:07.372
that we have to go through all this at the crack of dawn just to get it?

554
37:07.456 --> 37:10.834
I closed the restaurant, cleaned the kitchen, cleaned the house,

555
37:10.917 --> 37:13.420
didn't get to sleep until after 3:00, and you woke me up at 5:00.

556
37:13.503 --> 37:16.423
Isn't that a little much? [sighs]

557
37:16.506 --> 37:20.052
Besides, we're shooting the stupid Round Two tomorrow.

558
37:20.135 --> 37:22.721
Why are we suddenly going hiking the day before?

559
37:23.889 --> 37:26.475
Do we really need whatever it is we're off to get right now?

560
37:58.215 --> 38:00.050
I have a bad feeling about this.

561
38:01.468 --> 38:04.471
This isn't a place where they brew snakes, is it?

562
38:05.055 --> 38:07.641
I hate snakes more than anything.

563
38:09.017 --> 38:10.435
[rustling]

564
38:12.062 --> 38:13.897
[intense music playing]

565
38:25.450 --> 38:27.410
[gasps]

566
38:32.916 --> 38:34.668
-[insect buzzing] -[screams]

567
38:36.670 --> 38:38.004
[screaming continues]

568
38:48.515 --> 38:49.724
Thank you, sir.

569
38:49.808 --> 38:51.810
-Sure, anytime. -Thanks.

570
39:00.569 --> 39:01.736
What's up?

571
39:01.820 --> 39:06.283
Oh. I'm just… I'm just not used to places like this.

572
39:07.159 --> 39:08.201
Let's go back.

573
39:10.287 --> 39:11.454
What do you have there?

574
39:12.164 --> 39:13.165
Oh, this?

575
39:13.248 --> 39:14.791
[chuckles]

576
39:14.875 --> 39:17.586
It's nuruk. Nuruk.

577
39:17.669 --> 39:18.670
Nuruk?

578
39:20.630 --> 39:23.717
[gasps] Wow. They're pretty.

579
39:26.595 --> 39:27.721
This is nuruk?

580
39:28.763 --> 39:31.766
Has nuruk always been this pretty? I had no idea.

581
39:33.310 --> 39:36.104
So… What are you planning to do with all of this?

582
39:37.689 --> 39:38.732
Hmm.

583
39:39.941 --> 39:42.611
Let's get back. We'll think about it then.

584
39:48.116 --> 39:49.743
Song. Let's go.

585
39:52.996 --> 39:55.665
[Captain Cook] Contestants, are you ready to cook up something

586
39:55.749 --> 39:57.417
exciting today?

587
39:57.500 --> 39:58.919
[all] Yes!

588
39:59.002 --> 40:00.795
[Captain Cook laughing]

589
40:01.504 --> 40:04.257
As I told you last week, the theme for this round

590
40:04.341 --> 40:06.176
is "Splendor in the Grass."

591
40:06.259 --> 40:07.302
SPLENDOR IN THE GRASS

592
40:07.385 --> 40:09.512
What kind of delicious and exciting dishes

593
40:09.596 --> 40:13.183
will the finest chefs gathered here today create?

594
40:16.269 --> 40:18.605
Da-dum, da-dum, da-dum, da-dum.

595
40:19.773 --> 40:21.733
Oh, I can't wait to find out.

596
40:21.816 --> 40:23.693
[chuckling]

597
40:24.361 --> 40:27.656
Now, then, for all our viewers who are curious, let's go ahead

598
40:28.240 --> 40:31.243
and reveal the challenge right away for you.

599
40:33.161 --> 40:35.538
MARBLING

600
40:36.122 --> 40:40.293
When you are buying beef, what's the first thing you usually look at?

601
40:41.378 --> 40:43.296
Many of you check the country of origin.

602
40:43.380 --> 40:45.548
Whether it's Hanwoo or imported,

603
40:45.632 --> 40:48.218
American or Australian beef, that's important.

604
40:48.760 --> 40:51.221
But isn't the grade just as important?

605
40:51.304 --> 40:53.473
Is it prime or choice?

606
40:53.556 --> 40:55.725
In other words, the marbling grade.

607
40:59.020 --> 41:03.441
Marbling refers to the amount of fat distributed within the lean meat.

608
41:03.525 --> 41:07.445
The more fat there is, the higher the marbling score will be.

609
41:10.365 --> 41:11.741
Why is he talking about marbling now?

610
41:13.076 --> 41:14.286
Don't tell me…

611
41:14.369 --> 41:17.956
Now, if you want your beef to have the highest marbling score,

612
41:18.039 --> 41:20.333
you need the cattle to accumulate more fat.

613
41:20.417 --> 41:23.461
What that means is that you must feed them large amounts of grain

614
41:24.045 --> 41:27.799
such as corn, rather than letting them graze on pasture.

615
41:27.882 --> 41:31.177
And because you need to reduce the muscle content,

616
41:31.261 --> 41:34.973
you keep them in small pens, so they can't exercise.

617
41:35.473 --> 41:39.602
What happens when the cattle is raised using that kind of method?

618
41:40.228 --> 41:43.440
Yes. Naturally, they develop health problems.

619
41:44.024 --> 41:46.609
So what do you have to do then?

620
41:46.693 --> 41:50.363
You give them antibiotics and various other injections.

621
41:50.947 --> 41:55.118
This kind of beef doesn't have omega-3 fatty acids that are good for us,

622
41:55.201 --> 42:00.582
but instead contains omega-6 fatty acids that are harmful to us.

623
42:00.665 --> 42:04.336
Yes. In fact, beef with a high marbling score

624
42:04.419 --> 42:07.255
is from cows that couldn't graze freely in the meadow

625
42:07.339 --> 42:09.632
and eat fresh grass.

626
42:09.716 --> 42:13.428
In my opinion, it is unhealthy meat.

627
42:15.138 --> 42:18.433
So, why do we like this kind of meat?

628
42:21.519 --> 42:22.437
Umami.

629
42:23.313 --> 42:26.483
It is because of the umami that comes from the marbling.

630
42:27.817 --> 42:31.780
It is the allure of that umami taste on our tongues

631
42:32.405 --> 42:35.075
that ultimately keeps us from giving up the fat.

632
42:36.409 --> 42:39.329
Korea, the US and Japan are the only three countries

633
42:39.412 --> 42:42.832
that judge the quality of beef according to its marbling.

634
42:43.500 --> 42:46.503
Therefore, I present to you today's challenge.

635
42:54.177 --> 42:55.470
MAXIMIZE UMAMI WITH PASTURE-RAISED,

636
42:55.553 --> 42:56.930
LOW-MARBLED CHIKSO BEEF FROM ULLEUNGDO!

637
43:05.230 --> 43:06.189
MAXIMIZE UMAMI WITH PASTURE-RAISED,

638
43:06.272 --> 43:07.273
LOW-MARBLED CHIKSO BEEF FROM ULLEUNGDO!

639
43:07.357 --> 43:08.817
[exhales shakily]

640
43:08.900 --> 43:10.485
[In-seop] We're screwed.

641
43:10.568 --> 43:14.489
Let me introduce our illustrious guest judges for this round.

642
43:14.572 --> 43:15.865
YOON MAN-SEOP, MEAT SUPPLIER

643
43:15.949 --> 43:17.367
OH JEONG-HO, HANWOO OMAKASE OWNER

644
43:17.450 --> 43:18.576
KIM JU-WAN, BBQ RESTAURANT OWNER

645
43:18.660 --> 43:20.161
GIUSEPPE LOMBARDO, ITALIAN MASTER BUTCHER

646
43:20.245 --> 43:22.080
MIN GI-JEONG, INFLUENCER MEATY LOVE, MEAT-LOVER CLUB

647
43:22.664 --> 43:26.918
[Woo-jin] Using big-data analysis, we came up with hundreds of possibilities.

648
43:27.001 --> 43:28.837
And one result that caught my eye

649
43:28.920 --> 43:31.506
linked the word "grassland" with pasture-raised cattle.

650
43:32.090 --> 43:36.261
I didn't expect the main ingredient to be low-marbled chikso beef from Ulleungdo.

651
43:36.344 --> 43:39.389
But I did think beef might be part of the challenge.

652
43:45.728 --> 43:46.813
The issue…

653
43:47.814 --> 43:48.898
is the umami.

654
43:48.982 --> 43:51.734
What can we do to maximize that umami?

655
43:52.735 --> 43:53.736
That's the key.

656
43:54.612 --> 43:57.031
[Joshua] The meat may have good texture,

657
43:57.115 --> 43:58.783
but the truth is it's not savory.

658
43:58.867 --> 44:02.120
We need to create umami with something like a sauce.

659
44:02.203 --> 44:03.788
But I chose to make a rub instead.

660
44:03.872 --> 44:06.249
Using different spices and herbs, I'll make a rub with a complex flavor…

661
44:06.332 --> 44:07.917
JOSHUA YEONG-HO PAYTON OWNER-CHEF OF HUSH

662
44:08.001 --> 44:10.420
…then rub it onto the meat.

663
44:10.503 --> 44:14.299
We're also going to make a Southern-style barbecue sauce for dipping.

664
44:14.382 --> 44:17.677
Of course, I'll add my own twist.

665
44:17.760 --> 44:20.221
Because this is K-food.

666
44:22.307 --> 44:24.851
AS THE WINNER OF ROUND 1, HOW DO YOU FEEL ABOUT ROUND 2?

667
44:24.934 --> 44:28.438
[Han] Uh… My team and I put a lot of thought into preparing for Round Two.

668
44:29.564 --> 44:32.233
I never expected the challenge to be marbling.

669
44:33.318 --> 44:37.363
But then, a really fun idea came to me.

670
44:37.447 --> 44:39.532
An idea both exciting and intriguing.

671
44:39.616 --> 44:40.825
CHRIS K. HEAD CHEF OF FAMIGLIA

672
44:40.909 --> 44:43.578
WE HEARD YOU PREPARED TO COOK PUFFER FISH…

673
44:43.661 --> 44:44.996
YOO IN-SEOP HEAD CHEF OF SEOP

674
44:45.079 --> 44:46.080
[sighs deeply]

675
45:00.762 --> 45:01.846
Perilla.

676
45:02.472 --> 45:03.765
Cheongyang peppers.

677
45:04.474 --> 45:05.642
Parsnips.

678
45:06.643 --> 45:08.061
And even super sweet corn.

679
45:08.895 --> 45:10.563
I don't know what you're trying to make there…

680
45:12.857 --> 45:14.567
but that's a bizarre combination.

681
45:20.448 --> 45:21.783
What do you two have?

682
45:24.118 --> 45:25.537
Kim Tae-seop and you.

683
45:28.748 --> 45:29.749
Chris K.

684
45:32.669 --> 45:33.836
Eight years ago…

685
45:35.964 --> 45:36.965
Paris…

686
45:39.884 --> 45:41.052
The cathedral.

687
45:45.974 --> 45:47.976
Specifically, at Tae-seop's funeral.

688
45:51.646 --> 45:53.398
What were you doing there?

689
45:55.692 --> 45:56.693
Huh?

690
45:57.443 --> 45:58.444
Over there.

691
46:02.073 --> 46:03.575
Looks like a serious conversation.

692
46:03.658 --> 46:05.868
Why are they just standing there and not in the kitchen?

693
46:05.952 --> 46:06.953
You're right.

694
46:07.036 --> 46:09.956
Man, the refrigerator motor is making it hard to pick up the audio.

695
46:10.039 --> 46:12.333
Why did they have to talk there, huh?

696
46:25.638 --> 46:26.639
Is that all?

697
46:31.978 --> 46:34.147
That can't be the only thing you wanna know.

698
46:51.414 --> 46:53.291
[exciting music playing]

699
47:10.767 --> 47:12.018
[chiming]

700
47:13.519 --> 47:14.771
Chef Geon-woo!

701
47:14.854 --> 47:15.897
Wait a second.

702
47:22.028 --> 47:24.072
Squeeze out the moisture. Wring it out.

703
47:24.155 --> 47:25.156
Yes, Chef.

704
47:25.239 --> 47:27.241
And add the brown rice oil while blending.

705
47:27.325 --> 47:29.494
-As thick as possible. Got it? -Yes, Chef.

706
47:33.790 --> 47:35.416
-Sous-chef. -Yes?

707
47:35.500 --> 47:37.919
Soak the rice first, and I'll get the smoking setup

708
47:38.002 --> 47:39.587
-with jujube and straw. -Yes, Chef.

709
47:40.672 --> 47:43.424
And the soaked rice needs to simmer over low heat for at least two hours.

710
47:43.508 --> 47:44.509
Yes, Chef!

711
47:46.010 --> 47:50.264
Song, add just enough ginger enzyme to get rid of the fishy smell, okay?

712
47:50.348 --> 47:51.349
Yes, Chef.

713
48:00.692 --> 48:01.693
Let me taste it.

714
48:10.702 --> 48:11.703
Wow.

715
48:15.206 --> 48:16.249
Yes!

716
48:17.375 --> 48:18.668
-Keep stirring it. -Okay.

717
48:56.831 --> 48:59.709
[mysterious music playing]

718
49:02.879 --> 49:04.297
[grunts]

719
49:08.551 --> 49:09.635
[gasps]

720
49:14.724 --> 49:17.059
[intense music playing]

721
49:18.269 --> 49:19.812
[gasping]

722
49:26.277 --> 49:29.322
[panting]

723
49:39.081 --> 49:40.500
[grunts, panting]

724
49:51.886 --> 49:54.472
[panting continues]

725
50:02.688 --> 50:04.649
I told you to make it thinner. Damn it!

726
50:06.484 --> 50:10.196
I said make the wheat crepes thin enough that you can see the filling through them!

727
50:10.279 --> 50:12.573
-Did I or did I not? -Yes, Chef.

728
50:14.283 --> 50:15.618
The whole point is

729
50:15.701 --> 50:17.870
that you should be able to tell what's inside at a glance.

730
50:17.954 --> 50:18.996
Yes, Chef.

731
50:19.080 --> 50:21.290
Did you put it straight into the ice bath?

732
50:21.374 --> 50:23.709
-Uh, well, the ice-- -The texture is ruined.

733
50:23.793 --> 50:25.419
I told you to put it straight into the ice!

734
50:25.503 --> 50:27.713
I'm sorry, Chef. We'll do it again.

735
50:29.131 --> 50:30.550
Everyone, focus!

736
50:32.635 --> 50:35.513
If even one component is off, the whole thing falls out of balance.

737
50:35.596 --> 50:37.557
-Yes, Chef! -Yes, Chef.

738
50:41.102 --> 50:42.478
Michael, how's the sabayon coming?

739
50:42.562 --> 50:45.648
Uh, It should be ready in about ten minutes. I'll add the gochujang then.

740
50:45.731 --> 50:47.942
Okay. If it needs more sweetness, add a little more grain syrup.

741
50:48.025 --> 50:48.901
Yes, Chef.

742
50:48.985 --> 50:51.737
-Yeong-su, grab a few Chinese yams. -Yes, Chef!

743
51:11.382 --> 51:13.551
VIP MONITORING ROOM

744
51:34.322 --> 51:37.617
I'm fine. You all can leave now. I'll call you if I need you.

745
52:12.735 --> 52:14.904
I told you all to leave. Are you deaf?

746
52:39.720 --> 52:41.973
Contestants, ten seconds remaining.

747
52:42.056 --> 52:43.182
Ten.

748
52:43.265 --> 52:44.600
Nine.

749
52:45.101 --> 52:46.185
Eight.

750
52:46.686 --> 52:47.687
Seven.

751
52:48.562 --> 52:50.022
Six.

752
52:50.106 --> 52:51.482
Five.

753
52:51.565 --> 52:52.858
Four.

754
52:52.942 --> 52:54.443
Three.

755
52:54.527 --> 52:55.569
Two.

756
52:55.653 --> 52:58.739
One. Contestants, your time is up now.

757
53:00.324 --> 53:03.119
HUSH: SOUTHERN US BBQ STEAK AND A POTATO PLAYGROUND

758
53:03.202 --> 53:06.038
I used a traditional Southern American cooking method

759
53:06.122 --> 53:08.165
and slow-grilled the meat over charcoal.

760
53:08.249 --> 53:11.293
I focused on boosting the umami by generously coating the meat

761
53:11.919 --> 53:14.213
with a rub made from various Korean spices and herbs.

762
53:14.880 --> 53:17.049
And nothing beats some fine potatoes

763
53:17.550 --> 53:18.926
as a side for steak.

764
53:19.552 --> 53:22.805
I fried and confited potatoes in various shapes and forms

765
53:23.305 --> 53:25.725
and gave it the name "Potato Playground."

766
53:26.308 --> 53:30.730
The reason I called it a playground comes from a personal memory of mine.

767
53:31.313 --> 53:35.109
It was inspired by a memory when I was eating cotton candy

768
53:35.192 --> 53:37.737
at a playground as I waited for my mother.

769
53:41.282 --> 53:42.700
This inspired me.

770
53:44.076 --> 53:46.954
ONGOJISIM BEEF NOODLES WITH CORN JANG

771
53:47.038 --> 53:51.000
Since the meat itself is lean and not especially rich in flavor,

772
53:51.083 --> 53:52.793
in order to boost the umami,

773
53:52.877 --> 53:56.839
I thought a light beef broth would work better than grilling or braising it.

774
53:57.548 --> 54:00.676
I happened to find some excellent super-sweet corn.

775
54:00.760 --> 54:03.679
So I made a corn jang using traditional soy sauce.

776
54:03.763 --> 54:08.225
I then paired it with the broth in order to further enhance the umami.

777
54:08.309 --> 54:10.061
I didn't have to use any tricks.

778
54:11.270 --> 54:13.814
I gave a traditional Korean dish

779
54:14.732 --> 54:16.192
a new interpretation.

780
54:17.568 --> 54:20.446
EXCEPTION: WHEAT CREPE CARPACCIO AND GOCHUJANG SABAYON

781
54:20.529 --> 54:21.947
We didn't have much time,

782
54:22.031 --> 54:26.744
so I used an unusual method to age and freeze the meat,

783
54:26.827 --> 54:28.913
basically as quickly as possible.

784
54:28.996 --> 54:34.001
In order to make carpaccio, the meat must be sliced extremely thin.

785
54:47.348 --> 54:51.894
The gochujang would be responsible for making up the meat's lack of umami.

786
54:52.478 --> 54:54.730
The ingredients in the gochujang are…

787
54:57.775 --> 54:59.193
Sorry, but that's a secret.

788
55:01.487 --> 55:02.947
FAMIGLIA: GRILLED SOUTH AMERICAN ASADO,

789
55:03.030 --> 55:04.406
PERILLA LEAF CHIMICHURRI, GRILLED CORN

790
55:04.990 --> 55:06.784
This is South American asado.

791
55:07.284 --> 55:08.911
I coated the cut with nuruk,

792
55:08.994 --> 55:11.038
aged it for an hour, and then grilled it.

793
55:12.456 --> 55:15.960
The sauce is a Korean variation on chimichurri.

794
55:43.988 --> 55:45.739
When people hear "nuruk,"

795
55:45.823 --> 55:47.867
they think it's only used to make alcohol.

796
55:48.450 --> 55:49.577
But did you know

797
55:49.660 --> 55:53.706
if you coat meat with nuruk, it boosts the umami dramatically?

798
55:53.789 --> 55:55.749
It also tenderizes the meat.

799
55:57.668 --> 56:02.006
Nuruk can also be used to make softer foods for older people to eat.

800
56:05.426 --> 56:06.969
Wow. [chuckling]

801
56:10.306 --> 56:12.850
Grilling meat coated with nuruk?

802
56:13.475 --> 56:14.518
[chuckles]

803
56:15.436 --> 56:17.271
That guy really is…

804
56:17.354 --> 56:18.731
WHAT IS THE JANGDOKDAE BESIDE YOU USED FOR?

805
56:18.814 --> 56:20.107
This guy?

806
56:21.442 --> 56:24.195
This is an oven that was handmade.

807
56:24.820 --> 56:26.572
It's a nuruk starter oven.

808
56:26.655 --> 56:30.117
The insides are repeatedly coated with sesame oil and nuruk salt.

809
56:30.951 --> 56:34.538
If you grill nuruk-coated meat over the grill, the nuruk burns quickly.

810
56:35.122 --> 56:39.168
So we use this, slowly layering on the aroma of the nuruk.

811
56:39.793 --> 56:40.794
The fire,

812
56:40.878 --> 56:43.797
we burn a mixture of straw and jujube branches.

813
56:45.299 --> 56:47.051
We cook the meat with that heat and smoke.

814
56:47.134 --> 56:49.094
ROUND 2 HIGHLIGHTS JUDGING IN PROGRESS

815
56:50.012 --> 56:51.639
Everyone looks confident.

816
56:51.722 --> 56:53.349
Good, very good.

817
56:53.974 --> 56:55.643
Yeah. Mmm.

818
56:55.726 --> 56:58.938
Well, I don't think Chef Cha is going to be eliminated this round.

819
56:59.021 --> 57:00.022
What do you think?

820
57:01.899 --> 57:02.900
Hmm.

821
57:04.526 --> 57:10.908
[sighs] But I think if Cha Woo-jin were eliminated further on in the final round,

822
57:11.575 --> 57:15.704
then, um, well, I think he'd be devastated.

823
57:18.249 --> 57:20.876
And if, by some slim chance,

824
57:21.919 --> 57:25.005
he happened to find out that the very person

825
57:25.631 --> 57:28.926
who kept him from winning was you, of all people.

826
57:31.595 --> 57:33.555
And if, by some chance,

827
57:34.598 --> 57:38.936
at the end of the day, he ended up facing Famiglia in the final…

828
57:41.146 --> 57:45.609
and lost yet another competition to Chris K., of all people,

829
57:46.735 --> 57:49.196
who absolutely crushed him before.

830
58:00.082 --> 58:01.875
[phone buzzing]

831
58:07.006 --> 58:08.007
Mmm.

832
58:10.426 --> 58:11.427
Really?

833
58:12.761 --> 58:14.013
All right.

834
58:16.140 --> 58:18.642
I should get going. Something came up that I need to review.

835
58:23.314 --> 58:24.732
Anyway, excuse me…

836
58:27.109 --> 58:28.110
Hae-seon.

837
58:41.457 --> 58:42.458
Mmm.

838
58:44.251 --> 58:47.338
So you managed to get photographs from his childhood?

839
58:49.256 --> 58:51.425
But are you sure that source can be trusted?

840
58:53.469 --> 58:54.470
All right.

841
58:55.304 --> 58:56.847
Come up to my office.

842
58:56.930 --> 58:57.931
Yeah, right now.

843
58:59.183 --> 59:00.100
ROUND 2 ELIMINATION

844
59:00.184 --> 59:03.812
The team to be eliminated in Round Two has been decided.

845
59:06.732 --> 59:09.109
[suspenseful music playing]

846
59:27.044 --> 59:29.463
The team eliminated this round is…

847
59:31.715 --> 59:32.841
[phone buzzing]

848
59:37.554 --> 59:39.264
PARK JI-YEON

849
59:39.348 --> 59:42.643
[Ji-Yeon] I asked around, and someone has a childhood photo of Chris K.

850
59:42.726 --> 59:45.938
that was posted to his high school's online forum years ago.

851
59:46.021 --> 59:49.024
They're sending it over now. I'll forward it as soon as I get it.

852
59:54.822 --> 59:56.990
The eliminated team…

853
01:00:07.960 --> 01:00:09.420
ROUND 2 ELIMINATED TEAM SEOP

854
01:00:13.465 --> 01:00:14.716
…is Team Seop.

855
01:00:37.156 --> 01:00:38.157
[buzzing]

856
01:00:38.240 --> 01:00:39.533
PARK JI-YEON

857
01:00:52.379 --> 01:00:55.215
ROUND 2 GUEST JUDGES' FINAL SCORECARD

858
01:01:08.687 --> 01:01:11.023
All right. Unfortunately,

859
01:01:11.607 --> 01:01:14.985
we won't be announcing a winning team for this round.

860
01:01:17.029 --> 01:01:20.407
[dramatic music playing]

861
01:01:23.160 --> 01:01:27.080
Well, sure, it is your call. But could you tell us why, Mr. Bernier?

862
01:01:27.164 --> 01:01:32.085
Yes. It just so happens that three teams have had the same score.

863
01:01:33.462 --> 01:01:37.216
We found that there isn't a single dominant team this round.

864
01:01:37.299 --> 01:01:41.261
So we'll announce the winning team in the next round the next week.

865
01:01:42.262 --> 01:01:43.263
Instead…

866
01:01:46.433 --> 01:01:49.061
we'll reveal the theme for Round Three now,

867
01:01:49.144 --> 01:01:51.688
which I'm sure you've all been waiting for.

868
01:01:58.904 --> 01:02:00.781
[breathing shakily]

869
01:02:04.451 --> 01:02:06.745
[dramatic music playing]

870
01:02:24.221 --> 01:02:26.306
YOU ARE MY DESTINY

871
01:02:29.893 --> 01:02:31.061
What…

872
01:02:31.144 --> 01:02:32.813
Wait, what does that mean?

873
01:02:33.480 --> 01:02:39.570
His father is Chairwoman Moon's husband, Kang San?

874
01:02:39.653 --> 01:02:40.654
Then…

875
01:02:42.447 --> 01:02:44.908
you mean he's the chairwoman's son?

876
01:02:46.034 --> 01:02:48.662
[gentle music playing]

877
01:03:03.719 --> 01:03:04.761
Not yet.

878
01:03:05.887 --> 01:03:07.347
It's not time yet.

879
01:03:10.434 --> 01:03:11.727
[security entrance beeps]

880
01:03:15.147 --> 01:03:16.148
Jeong-suk.

881
01:03:17.649 --> 01:03:19.526
-Hey. -Good work.

882
01:03:20.527 --> 01:03:23.655
Wow, this round was really fun too. You did great.

883
01:03:23.739 --> 01:03:25.282
[chuckles] Thanks.

884
01:03:26.366 --> 01:03:28.076
Um… Hae-seon.

885
01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:30.162
-Mmm? -Uh…

886
01:03:31.413 --> 01:03:33.749
What did you think of this round's dishes?

887
01:03:34.541 --> 01:03:36.001
[Hae-seon] They were excellent.

888
01:03:36.543 --> 01:03:40.172
Oh, and the three dishes that tied. Which were they?

889
01:03:40.255 --> 01:03:42.716
[Jeong-suk] Oh. Ah, those?

890
01:03:43.467 --> 01:03:44.468
Well…

891
01:03:51.308 --> 01:03:52.309
[groans]

892
01:03:52.392 --> 01:03:54.227
-How's everything, by the way? -[chuckles]

893
01:03:54.311 --> 01:03:56.688
-Anything I can help you with? -You've already…

894
01:04:16.333 --> 01:04:18.210
[gasping]

895
01:04:19.628 --> 01:04:20.671
[Jeong-suk gasps]

896
01:04:24.049 --> 01:04:25.175
Oh, Chris!

897
01:04:26.510 --> 01:04:27.719
-Chris! -Chris.

898
01:04:28.303 --> 01:04:29.513
-Oh, no. -Chris?

899
01:04:29.596 --> 01:04:30.722
Chris, please wake up.

900
01:04:30.806 --> 01:04:31.807
Chris?

901
01:04:32.391 --> 01:04:34.184
Chris… Chris, can you hear me?

902
01:04:34.768 --> 01:04:35.769
Chris!

903
01:04:35.852 --> 01:04:37.771
Chris, wake up. Chris!

904
01:04:37.854 --> 01:04:39.690
[Hae-seon] Chris. Chris?

905
01:04:40.273 --> 01:04:41.900
Chris, come on. Wake up.

906
01:04:43.985 --> 01:04:45.696
[no audible dialogue]

907
01:04:52.536 --> 01:04:53.912
[Hae-seon] Chris.

908
01:04:53.995 --> 01:04:54.996
Chris?

909
01:04:56.832 --> 01:04:58.709
Chris, can you hear me?

910
01:05:04.381 --> 01:05:06.049
[pop song playing]

911
01:05:35.579 --> 01:05:38.415
[Song-yi] Are you really okay?

912
01:05:39.499 --> 01:05:41.835
[Hyeon-ju] I thought he had died back then.

913
01:05:41.918 --> 01:05:43.754
He's grown up quite well.

914
01:05:43.837 --> 01:05:46.757
The restaurant's closed. I can take a day to clear my head, right?

915
01:05:47.716 --> 01:05:49.885
-[Han] No, no way. Ah… -[Song-yi] Can I go with you?

916
01:05:50.385 --> 01:05:53.263
[Han] Not on you, not on Famiglia.

917
01:05:53.346 --> 01:05:55.223
Let's not give up.

918
01:05:55.307 --> 01:05:57.684
[Song-yi] Please, tell me, Chris.

919
01:05:57.768 --> 01:05:59.811
What does this competition mean to you?

920
01:06:00.812 --> 01:06:02.898
[Hyeon-ju] To meet again like this.

921
01:06:03.482 --> 01:06:04.483
[Han] Destiny.
